{"tag":"PSHT","body":"<div class=\"pq-q ue-math\"><p>This problem follows Rubinstein (2002). Adam’s only food in the Garden of Eden is apples. Time runs in discrete days <span class=\"m-nb\">(<span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"t=1,2,…\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8389em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">t</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"minner\">…</span></span></span></span></span></span>),</span> apples are indivisible units, and Adam ranks streams of daily apple consumption. Suppose:</p></div><ol class=\"pq-parts\" role=\"list\"><li class=\"pq-part\"><span class=\"pq-l\" aria-label=\"Part (a)\">a</span><div class=\"pq-pq\"><div class=\"ue-math\"><p>up to 2 apples per day he enjoys; 3 apples per day he finds unbearable;</p></div></div></li><li class=\"pq-part\"><span class=\"pq-l\" aria-label=\"Part (b)\">b</span><div class=\"pq-pq\"><div class=\"ue-math\"><p>he is impatient: giving up an apple promised for the following day in order to raise day <span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"t\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6151em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">t</span></span></span></span></span></span> consumption from 0 to 1, or from 1 to 2, pleases him;</p></div></div></li><li class=\"pq-part\"><span class=\"pq-l\" aria-label=\"Part (c)\">c</span><div class=\"pq-pq\"><div class=\"ue-math\"><p>on a day with no apple, he ranks 1 apple now above 2 apples the next day;</p></div></div></li><li class=\"pq-part\"><span class=\"pq-l\" aria-label=\"Part (d)\">d</span><div class=\"pq-pq\"><div class=\"ue-math\"><p>his expected lifespan is 120 years.</p><p>Prove that, offered 2 apples per day from day 4 until the end of his expected life, Adam would trade this offer for a single apple now.</p></div></div></li></ol><div class=\"pq-ans pq-ans--whole\" data-answer><p class=\"pq-ans-k\">Answer</p><div class=\"ue-math\"><p>Shown, for a life of any finite length <span class=\"m-nb\"><span class=\"katex-lite\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"T\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">T</span></span></span></span></span></span>:</span> Adam weakly prefers one apple right away to two apples a day from day 4 to the end of his life, so he would accept the exchange.</p></div></div>"}